Rechecked the original example with Day.js 1.11.23 on Node 24.19.0 / ICU 78.3. With host `TZ=UTC`, `America/Santiago`, `America/New_York` or `Asia/Shanghai`, the result is still `2020-10-25T23:00:00.000Z`. A `Europe/London` host produces the expected `2020-10-26T00:00:00.000Z`, so that environment can hide the discrepancy. Also, the London fall-back day in this example is 25 hours long; the two calendar days span 49 hours.

For applications that can accept `@js-temporal/polyfill@0.5.1`, an explicit wall-clock calculation is another option:

```js
import { Temporal } from '@js-temporal/polyfill';

const zone = 'Europe/London';
const start = Temporal.Instant.from('2020-10-23T23:00:00Z');
const local = start.toZonedDateTimeISO(zone).toPlainDateTime();
const result = local.add({ days: 2 }).toZonedDateTime(zone, {
  disambiguation: 'reject',
});
console.log(result.toInstant().toString()); // 2020-10-26T00:00:00Z
```

`reject` makes a missing or repeated target time explicit; `earlier`, `later` and `compatible` are available if adjustment is intended. A calendar shift and a fixed elapsed duration need different semantics.

I put a [free MIT helper and runnable checks here](https://github.com/tevinch/data-shape-kit/tree/main/examples/calendar-shift), including Day.js input/output, signed day/week/month/year shifts, month-end behavior, milliseconds and five host time zones. The guide includes the existing upstream proposals. This is an application-level option with an extra dependency, and it uses the runtime's timezone data.

